# Task: Analyse Existing Implementation and Prepare Technical Documentation

Act as a Senior Solution Architect and Technical Documentation Engineer.

I want you to prepare comprehensive technical documentation for the implementation available in the current workspace/repository.

The documentation will eventually be published as a Confluence Wiki Page and maintained alongside the implementation in Git.

## Important Instructions

This is an existing implementation, not a new architecture design exercise.

Your responsibility is to understand what has actually been implemented and document it accurately.

Follow these rules strictly:

1. Do not assume how the architecture works.
2. Do not invent components, workflows, scripts, configurations, features, or capabilities.
3. Do not generate example implementations before inspecting the actual implementation.
4. Do not replace actual implementation details with generic best practices.
5. Do not claim that a feature exists unless you can verify it in the source code.
6. If something is unclear or cannot be verified, identify it as an open question.
7. Do not modify any application code, workflows, configurations, or implementation files.
8. Do not expose actual secrets, credentials, tokens, sensitive Vault paths, or confidential organisation information.

## Phase 1: Discover and Understand the Implementation

First, inspect the workspace and identify all relevant implementation files.

Explore the actual:

- Repository structure.
- GitHub Actions workflows.
- Reusable workflows.
- Custom Composite Actions.
- YAML configuration files.
- Secrets-manifest.yaml and its schema.
- HashiCorp Vault integration.
- Authentication and authorization mechanisms.
- Secret retrieval and delivery implementation.
- Supporting shell scripts or utilities.
- Java automation framework.
- JSON readers.
- Properties file readers.
- Test-data loading mechanisms.
- Maven execution.
- Reporting and cleanup mechanisms.
- Existing documentation.

Trace dependencies and execution flow rather than relying on filenames alone.

Identify how the components interact and what responsibilities they actually perform.

Pay particular attention to:

- How the application repository communicates with the centralized implementation.
- How Secrets-manifest.yaml is interpreted and consumed.
- How custom Composite Actions are constructed and invoked.
- How test data moves from Vault into the runtime environment.
- How the existing automation framework consumes the delivered test data.
- What changes were made to existing JSON and Properties readers.
- Whether existing test scenarios and interfaces were preserved.

Do not start writing the final documentation yet.

## Phase 2: Present Your Findings and Proposed Documentation Structure

After analysing the implementation, present a discovery report containing:

### A. Implementation Inventory

List the relevant source files, workflows, Composite Actions, scripts, configurations, and Java classes.

For each item, explain its actual responsibility and how you verified it.

Include repository-relative file paths.

### B. Reconstructed Architecture

Explain the architecture you discovered.

Describe the actual component relationships and execution sequence.

Identify the boundaries between:

- Centralized orchestration.
- Application repository configuration.
- Reusable workflow execution.
- Composite Actions.
- Vault authentication and secret retrieval.
- Manifest interpretation.
- Runtime data delivery.
- Automation framework integration.
- Reporting and cleanup.

Only include components that actually exist.

### C. Secret Manifest Contract

Explain the actual structure and purpose of Secrets-manifest.yaml.

Identify its fields, data types, validation rules, delivery options, and how it interacts with the centralized workflow.

Explain the distinction between configuration requirements and security authorization.

### D. Framework Integration

Explain how the existing JSON and Properties readers work.

Identify the actual changes introduced to support the new test-data delivery mechanism.

Describe the impact on existing test code and backward compatibility, where verifiable.

### E. Open Questions and Gaps

List implementation details that cannot be established confidently from the available source.

Do not fill gaps with assumptions.

### F. Proposed Confluence Documentation Structure

Recommend a logical documentation structure based on the actual implementation.

For every proposed section, explain:

- What it will cover.
- Why it is necessary.
- Which source files support it.
- Whether it needs code snippets, tables, architecture diagrams, sequence diagrams, or operational instructions.

Also recommend whether the content should be one comprehensive Wiki page or multiple related Wiki pages.

## Phase 3: Agree on Documentation Format

Before writing the final documentation, propose the following:

1. Documentation title.
2. Intended audience.
3. Executive summary format.
4. Architecture diagram format.
5. End-to-end sequence diagram format.
6. Component-level documentation format.
7. Composite Action documentation format.
8. Secrets-manifest.yaml contract reference format.
9. Vault integration documentation format.
10. JSON and Properties reader before/after comparison format.
11. Code snippet presentation format.
12. Operational runbook format.
13. Onboarding guide format.
14. Security and governance documentation format.
15. Future enhancement and limitations format.

Prefer Mermaid diagrams for architecture and execution flows where appropriate.

Recommend suitable tables for configuration contracts, inputs/outputs, component responsibilities, and error handling.

Use real code snippets from the implementation, with source file references.

Clearly distinguish:
- Implemented functionality.
- Partially implemented functionality.
- Recommended improvements.
- Open questions.

## Phase 4: Wait for My Approval

Do not generate the final documentation or README.md at this stage.

Present the discovery report and proposed documentation structure first.

Wait for my review, corrections, and approval.

Only after approval should you generate the final Confluence-ready documentation and Markdown files.

## Expected Quality

The final documentation should be:

- Technically accurate.
- Traceable to the actual implementation.
- Suitable for enterprise engineering teams.
- Understandable to architects, SDETs, developers, DevOps, and engineering leadership.
- Rich in relevant code snippets and diagrams.
- Maintainable as the implementation evolves.
- Free from invented architecture and unsupported claims.

The objective is to document the engineering solution that has actually been built, not to redesign or idealise it.
